[al:新概念英语（三）]
[ar:MP3 同步字幕版（美音）]
[ti:Nothing to Sell and Nothing to Buy]
[by:更多学习内容，请到VeryCD.com搜索“露珠”]
[00:00.30]Lesson 27 | 第27课
[00:02.43]Nothing to sell and nothing to buy | 不卖也不买
[00:11.24]What is the most important thing for a tramp? | 对流浪汉来说，最重要的东西是什么？
[00:16.99]It has been said that everyone lives by selling something. | 据说每个人都靠出售某种东西来维持生活。
[00:21.30]In the light of this statement, teachers live by selling knowledge, | 根据这种说法，教师靠出售知识为生，
[00:25.93]philosophers by selling wisdom and priests by selling spiritual comfort. | 哲学家靠出售智慧为生，牧师靠出售精神安慰为生。
[00:31.97]Though it may be possible to measure the value of material goods in terms of money, | 虽然物质产品的价值可以用金钱来衡量，
[00:38.43]it is extremely difficult to estimate the true value of the services which people perform for us. | 但要估算别人为我们提供的服务的真正价值却极其困难。
[00:46.57]There are times when we would willingly give everything we possess to save our lives, | 有时，为了挽救我们的生命，我们愿意付出自己的一切，
[00:52.85]yet we might grudge paying a surgeon a high fee for offering us precisely this service. | 但外科医生恰恰提供了这种服务，我们却可能不愿付给他高昂的手术费。
[01:00.36]The conditions of society are such that skills have to be paid for in the same way that goods are paid for at a shop. | 社会上的情况就是如此：技术必须像商店里的商品一样付钱购买。
[01:08.79]Everyone has something to sell. | 人人都有东西可卖。
[01:12.01]Tramps seem to be the only exception to this general rule. | 流浪汉似乎是这条普遍规律的唯一例外。
[01:16.71]Beggars almost sell themselves as human beings to arouse the pity of passers-by. | 乞丐几乎是把自己当作人来出卖，以唤起路人的怜悯。
[01:23.13]But real tramps are not beggars. | 但真正的流浪汉不是乞丐。
[01:25.62]They have nothing to sell and require nothing from others. | 他们既无可出卖，也无所求于人。
[01:30.15]In seeking independence, they do not sacrifice their human dignity. | 在追求独立的同时，他们并不牺牲做人的尊严。
[01:35.55]A tramp may ask you for money, but he will never ask you to feel sorry for him. | 流浪汉可能会向你要钱，但他从来不要你可怜他。
[01:41.54]He has deliberately chosen to lead the life he leads and is fully aware of the consequences. | 他是故意选择过那种生活的，并且完全清楚其后果。
[01:49.15]He may never be sure where the next meal is coming from, | 他也许永远不知道下一顿饭从哪儿来，
[01:53.69]but he is free from the thousands of anxieties which afflict other people. | 但他没有其他人所遭受的千百种焦虑。
[01:59.99]His few material possessions make it possible for him to move from place to place with ease. | 他仅有的几件物质财产使他能轻松地从一地迁往另一地。
[02:07.89]By having to sleep in the open, he gets far closer to the world of nature than most of us ever do. | 由于不得不露宿野外，他比我们中任何人都更接近大自然。
[02:16.18]He may hunt, beg, or steal occasionally to keep himself alive; | 为了活下去，他偶尔可能打猎、乞讨，甚至偷窃；
[02:21.89]he may even, in times of real need, do a little work; but he will never sacrifice his freedom. | 在真正急需时，他甚至可能干点活，但他决不会牺牲自由。
[02:30.07]We often speak of tramps with contempt and put them in the same class as beggars, | 我们谈起流浪汉时常带有轻蔑之意，把他们与乞丐归为一类，
[02:36.15]but how many of us can honestly say that we have not felt a little envious of their simple way of life and their freedom from care? | 但我们当中能有多少人坦率地说，对他们的简朴生活与无忧无虑没有一点羡慕呢？
